7] [17592186534877 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186534571 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534877 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "4ea4a547-6ad1-5f93-999f-8abb84e0f98d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534927 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186534331 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534927 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186534391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534927 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186534481 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534927 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186534588 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534927 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186534637 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534927 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534927 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534927 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186534331 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534927 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ab27c17a-102b-5532-a4af-ffd4b651eaf8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534929 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186534371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534929 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186534506 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534929 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186534536 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534929 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186534611 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534929 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186534826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534929 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534929 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534929 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186534826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534929 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "abe81e71-7378-51b7-8cd0-c1c7afdebd22" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534940 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186534344 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534940 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186534584 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534940 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186534591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534940 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186534694 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534940 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186534734 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534940 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534940 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534940 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186534584 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534940 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "bd4d08a8-464b-5031-bdac-129c93989f3b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535000 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535000 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125496" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535000 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535000 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/a5/a5f23af5dd514a65937eee29bb0992ff94d68897d8c6422fcf215aad45379c4a.png)\n\nConsider the part of parabola and constant vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the part of parabola is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n\nII. ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$\n\nIII. ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535000 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3f935fec-3b1f-581f-b980-6cf035478e31" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535000 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statements I is false, while statement II is true. As the particle moves from $A$ to $B,$ the force $F$ works with the particle for $y<0,$ while $F$  works against the particle for $y>0.$ \n\n![](images/53/537aca6190fc2f3e06e85c0781a8bb461559ef0a7515ee29aca505f9d615f820.png)\n\n \n\nBy symmetry, the contribution to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ for $y<0$ is canceled out by the contribution for $y>0.$ So,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $F$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](images/49/4939f9a22400a7a56eaeacb77d520e522f50aa8378ca1014a19810122e12da51.png)\n\n \n\nSo, ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535015 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534940 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535015 #edb/ref 1069 "q-150013" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535015 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535015 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/dc/dcf7a2ea5b44f3f5ba7b1663f7979cdc3fc8c1036b6cc797904ffda77f1003bd.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the сurve is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n\nII. ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$\n\nIII. ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535015 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5c3b3b24-43f3-512c-b4aa-af0e4524c252" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535015 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true while statement II is false. It's clear from the picture below that $F$  assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/81/818ad935fd7aa592f00c23c9c77799d127449b4bb6ffacee4354e7acf39887d8.png)\n\nSo, ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/70/70e268a08195a92573f41927ae7de653837fca273f0c75cb951243a31ec660a2.png)\n\nSo, ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535078 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535078 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125502" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535078 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535078 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/8a/8aed400e7768b70f4532642418d83b36d9e32aa84db87c71ea6d902504930bc4.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path where the semiellipse is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n\nII. ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$\n\nIII. ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535078 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e8382e90-f8cf-5c02-a1ba-6dd0a94124ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535078 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion  of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. Since the force $F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/d8/d8e05de34bf9ddfdbf9580b1df32eb9e091f56f76ac3616cbb1cd81d5d212a86.png)\n\n \nSo, ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $F$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](images/17/17423916013677c1476df5a4368ab9eec81671b04b4ca29a0e702b20ddc818f2.png)\n\n \nSo, ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535079 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534877 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535079 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125694" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535079 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535079 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/3c/3c2035d50b823b9bcbeb3ae8cf698df9a25c95a289c5a6f35054269dc920088d.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path where the curve is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n\nII. ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$\n\nIII. ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535079 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e8988e8d-780c-5cf1-8b3a-d358392fe42f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535079 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/df/dfec76b47e8508a57e18440a72986bcae8e2bd19559bd0915ea4530e0b08b4f6.png)\n\n \nSo, ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. It's clear from the picture below that if we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/58/58e3efc39d0aee02d14e7c80c7355c37a1f28b6f0f54ff7102553e3a6ffc17cf.png)\n\n \nSo, ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
